title: PolyAl
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

What is left of a [[Tetra Pak]] carton after the paper has been soaked out: [[Polyethylene]] and [[Aluminum]], still stuck to each other.

Tetra Pak's own word for it.

It is hard to pull apart, so mostly it isn't. It gets pressed into boards and pallets ([[Downcycling]]), burned for energy, or landfilled.

Tetra Pak reported 1.2 million tonnes of cartons collected for recycling in 2022, and more than 100,000 tonnes of PolyAl sent on to recyclers.

Sources:

* [[Tetra Pak Sustainability Report FY22: Collection & Recycling|https://csrwire.com/press_releases/786971-tetra-pak-sustainability-report-fy22-collection-recycling]]
